request.prompt:
Job S04: risk_scoring.sas (sas), file estate/sas/S04_risk_scoring.sas.
Customer risk score and tier from claims experience.

Code:
  1 | /******************************************************************************
  2 |   Program  : risk_scoring.sas
  3 |   Purpose  : Customer risk score and referral tier by line of business for
  4 |              underwriting. Claims experience (claim count, recoveries and
  5 |              severity against the line's average) plus the line's loss ratio,
  6 |              loaded for the policyholder's age and county.
  7 |   Inputs   : dw.claims_enriched, rpt.premium_band_summary, dw.dim_customer
  8 |   Output   : rpt.risk_scores (rebuilt on every run)
  9 |   Params   : as_of - reporting date (SAS date), set by the scheduler
 10 |   Author   : TF
 11 | 
 12 |   Change log
 13 |     2015-06-02  TF    Initial version (motor only)
 14 |     2016-11-21  TF    Home and travel added; scoring per line in a macro
 15 |                       (CR-0998)
 16 |     2018-04-09  GH    Age and county loadings from the pricing review (CR-1302)
 17 |     2020-01-27  GH    Line loss ratio from premium_bands.sas (CR-1544)
 18 |     2022-09-12  TF    Referral tiers for the underwriting queue (CR-2011)
 19 | ******************************************************************************/
 20 | 
 21 | /* policyholder loadings: age at the reporting date and county */
 22 | data work.cust(keep=customer_id age age_load county_load);
 23 |   set dw.dim_customer(keep=customer_id date_of_birth county);
 24 |   age = int((&as_of - date_of_birth) / 365.25);
 25 |   if missing(age) then age_load = 1.00;
 26 |   else if age < 25 then age_load = 1.40;
 27 |   else if age >= 70 then age_load = 1.25;
 28 |   else age_load = 1.00;
 29 |   select (upcase(strip(county)));
 30 |     when ('DUBLIN') county_load = 1.15;
 31 |     when ('CORK', 'LIMERICK', 'GALWAY') county_load = 1.05;
 32 |     otherwise county_load = 1.00;
 33 |   end;
 34 | run;
 35 | 
 36 | /* claims experience per customer and line of business */
 37 | proc sql;
 38 |   create table work.experience as
 39 |   select customer_id, lob_code,
 40 |          count(*)          as n_claims,
 41 |          sum(total_paid)   as paid,
 42 |          sum(n_recoveries) as n_recoveries
 43 |   from   dw.claims_enriched
 44 |   group  by customer_id, lob_code;
 45 | quit;
 46 | 
 47 | /* loss ratio per line: paid on the claims book over earned premium */
 48 | proc sql;
 49 |   create table work.lob_ratio as
 50 |   select e.lob_code, sum(e.paid) / p.earned as loss_ratio
 51 |   from   work.experience e,
 52 |          (select lob_code, sum(earned_eur) as earned
 53 |           from   rpt.premium_band_summary
 54 |           group  by lob_code) p
 55 |   where  e.lob_code = p.lob_code
 56 |   group  by e.lob_code, p.earned;
 57 | quit;
 58 | 
 59 | /* each customer against the average for the line */
 60 | %macro score_lines(lobs);
 61 |   %local i lob;
 62 |   %do i = 1 %to %sysfunc(countw(&lobs));
 63 |     %let lob = %scan(&lobs, &i);
 64 |     proc sql;
 65 |       create table work.score_&lob as
 66 |       select x.customer_id, x.lob_code, x.n_claims, x.paid, x.n_recoveries,
 67 |              x.paid / mean(x.paid) as severity
 68 |       from   work.experience x
 69 |       where  x.lob_code = "&lob";
 70 |     quit;
 71 |   %end;
 72 | %mend score_lines;
 73 | 
 74 | %score_lines(MOT HOM TRV);
 75 | 
 76 | proc sql;
 77 |   create table work.scoring as
 78 |   select s.*, c.age, c.age_load, c.county_load, r.loss_ratio
 79 |   from   (select * from work.score_mot
 80 |           union all
 81 |           select * from work.score_hom
 82 |           union all
 83 |           select * from work.score_trv) s
 84 |          left join work.cust c on c.customer_id = s.customer_id
 85 |          left join work.lob_ratio r on r.lob_code = s.lob_code
 86 |   order  by s.customer_id, s.lob_code;
 87 | quit;
 88 | 
 89 | /* points, loadings and the referral tier */
 90 | data rpt.risk_scores(keep=customer_id lob_code n_claims paid n_recoveries severity age
 91 |                           age_load county_load loss_ratio points score tier as_of_date);
 92 |   set work.scoring;
 93 |   sev_points = 0;
 94 |   if severity >= 2 then sev_points = 10;
 95 |   else if severity >= 1 then sev_points = 5;
 96 |   lob_points = round(2.5 * loss_ratio, 0.5);
 97 |   points = 12.5 * min(n_claims, 4) + 7.5 * n_recoveries + sev_points + lob_points;
 98 |   score  = round(points * age_load * county_load, 0.01);
 99 |   if score >= 35 then tier = 'HIGH';
100 |   else if score >= 22 then tier = 'MEDIUM';
101 |   else tier = 'LOW';
102 |   format as_of_date yymmdd10.;
103 |   as_of_date = &as_of;
104 | run;

Input tables:
- dw.claims_enriched: claim_id num 8 format=BEST, claim_number char 20 format=$CHAR, policy_id num 8 format=BEST, customer_id num 8 format=BEST, lob_code char 5 format=$CHAR, loss_date num 8 format=YYMMDD, reported_date num 8 format=YYMMDD, status char 12 format=$CHAR, n_payments num 8, n_recoveries num 8, total_paid num 8, indemnity_amt num 8, expense_amt num 8, recovery_amt num 8, first_payment_date num 8 format=DATE, last_payment_date num 8 format=DATE, recovery_flag num 8, has_payment num 8, gross_paid num 8, net_paid num 8
- rpt.premium_band_summary: lob_code char 5 format=$CHAR, prem_band char 8, n_policies num 8, n_transactions num 8, written_eur num 8 format=BEST, earned_eur num 8 format=BEST, as_of_date num 8 format=YYMMDD
- dw.dim_customer: customer_id INT, first_name NVARCHAR(40), last_name NVARCHAR(60), full_name NVARCHAR(101), initials NVARCHAR(2), email VARCHAR(120), email_shared_count INT, date_of_birth DATE, age_at_join INT, address_line1 NVARCHAR(100), town NVARCHAR(50), county VARCHAR(30), region VARCHAR(12), eircode VARCHAR(10), eircode_valid CHAR(1), segment_code CHAR(4), is_standard_segment CHAR(1), marketing_opt_in CHAR(1), customer_since DATE, source_system VARCHAR(10), version_count INT, record_updated_at DATETIME2(0), as_of_date DATE, _row_seq num 8

Output tables:
- rpt.risk_scores: customer_id num 8 format=BEST, lob_code char 5 format=$CHAR, n_claims num 8, paid num 8, n_recoveries num 8, severity num 8, age num 8, age_load num 8, county_load num 8, loss_ratio num 8, points num 8, score num 8, tier char 4, as_of_date num 8 format=YYMMDD

Trap cards:
- SAS-01 (semantic) A missing value is smaller than any number. Legacy: In SAS comparisons a missing numeric value is smaller than every number: if x < 0 is true when x is missing, and so is x <= 5; x > 0 is false. Missing sorts first. Spark: A comparison with NULL is NULL, which a filter or when() treats as false.
- SAS-02 (semantic) SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them. Legacy: sum(a, b) skips missing arguments (sum(., 2) = 2; all missing gives missing); a + b is missing if either is. The sum statement x + y; treats a missing y as 0 and x is retained. Spark: a + b is NULL if either is NULL; F.sum over rows skips NULLs; there is no row-wise sum().
- SAS-03 (semantic) Dates are numbers of days since 1 January 1960. Legacy: A SAS date is a number: days since 1960-01-01. Date arithmetic is plain arithmetic (d + 30, d2 - d1, int((d2 - d1) / 365.25)); a date compared with a number compares day counts. Datetimes are seconds since 1960-01-01T00:00. Spark: Spark dates are a separate type; arithmetic needs date functions.
- SAS-04 (semantic) INTCK and INTNX count interval boundaries. Legacy: intck('month', d1, d2) counts month boundaries crossed (like DATEDIFF), unless the 'continuous' method is given; intnx('month', d, n) moves to the start of the interval by default ('beginning'), or 'end'/'same' with the alignment argument. Spark: months_between gives fractions; add_months keeps the day; neither counts boundaries.
- SAS-05 (semantic) MERGE BY with IN= flags. Legacy: merge a(in=x) b(in=y); by k; matches rows by key one-to-one in order within each key; in= flags say which data sets contributed; variables from b overwrite a's for the same name; a key in only one data set keeps the other's variables missing. Many-to-many merges pair rows in order rather than forming a product. Spark: A join forms the product of matching rows; columns don't overwrite.
- SAS-06 (semantic) RETAIN and the sum statement, in input order. Legacy: retain keeps a variable's value across iterations of the DATA step; the sum statement x + y; is retained automatically, starts at 0 and ignores missing y. With BY groups, the code resets at first.key. The result depends on the order the rows are read. Spark: Spark has no row order and no carried state.
- SAS-07 (construct) FIRST. and LAST. variables. Legacy: With a BY statement, first.k is 1 on the first row of each k group and last.k on the last, in the data set's order. Spark: Spark has no implicit order; groups need a window over a stated order.
- SAS-08 (semantic) LAG inside a conditional. Legacy: lag(x) returns the value x had the last time that lag call executed, not the previous row; inside an IF it skips rows where the condition was false. Spark: F.lag over a window always returns the previous row's value.
- SAS-09 (semantic) NODUPKEY keeps the first row in input order. Legacy: proc sort nodupkey; by k; keeps the first row of each k in the input order (the sort is stable) and drops the rest; nodup compares whole rows with the previous one. Spark: dropDuplicates keeps an arbitrary row.
- SAS-10 (semantic) PROC SQL remerges summary statistics. Legacy: In PROC SQL, a SELECT that mixes summary functions (mean, sum, count, ...) with columns not in a GROUP BY computes the statistic over the whole table (or the group) and joins it back to every row ("The query requires remerging summary statistics"); with WHERE, over the filtered rows. Spark: Spark refuses such a query; the statistic must be a window or a join.
- SAS-11 (semantic) A character variable's length is fixed by its first assignment. Legacy: Without a LENGTH statement, a character variable gets the length of the first value assigned to it in the step (at compile time): if the first assignment is 'BIG', a later 'SMALL' is silently truncated to 'SMA'. Values are blank-padded to that length. Spark: Spark strings have no fixed length and never truncate.
- SAS-12 (semantic) PROC FORMAT range boundaries. Legacy: In a PROC FORMAT value statement, 'a - b' includes both ends, 'a <- b' excludes a, 'a -< b' excludes b; low and high are open ends; overlapping ranges resolve to the first match; values matching no range print as themselves (or the OTHER label). Missing values match no numeric range except '.'. Spark: A CASE WHEN must spell each boundary out.
- SAS-13 (construct) Macro loops. Legacy: %macro / %do loops generate repeated code at compile time, one copy per iteration, with macro variables substituted (&lob becomes MOT, HOM, ...). Spark: The generated copies become a Python loop or a single grouped query.
- SAS-14 (construct) PROC MEANS / SUMMARY with CLASS and NWAY. Legacy: proc means with class variables computes statistics for every combination of class levels; NWAY keeps only the full combination (_TYPE_ highest); missing class values are excluded unless MISSING is given; output variables are named by the OUTPUT statement. Spark: A groupBy over the class variables, which keeps NULL groups.
- SAS-15 (semantic) ROUND to a unit, with fuzz, on computed values; everything is an 8-byte double. Legacy: SAS numbers are 8-byte doubles. round(x, unit) rounds to the nearest multiple of the unit (round(2.85, 1) is 3, round(x, 0.5) to halves), half away from zero, and applies a fuzz factor so a computed value a tiny bit below a half (e.g. 2.675 computed as 2.67499999...) still rounds up as if it were exactly half. Spark: F.round(x, n) takes a number of decimal places, not a unit, and on a double rounds the binary value, so a computed 2.675 rounds to 2.67.
- SAS-16 (semantic) SAS's calendar has no 29 February in years divisible by 4000. Legacy: SAS treats years divisible by 4000 as non-leap, so day counts after 4000-02-28 are one day short per such year: '31DEC9999'd is day 2936547, where Python and Spark count 2936549 days from 1960-01-01. Spark: Spark follows the proleptic Gregorian calendar.

